Q: Returning values from exception handlers in Raku A: <p>(I would rather recommend against <em>wanting</em> such a thing in an actual code base - but Raku isn't going to tell you that so here we go.)</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>my $result = do given try { might-go-wonky() }, $! {
    when *, X::AdHoc { &quot;Ad-hoc exception thrown: $_[1]&quot; }
    when *, Exception { &quot;Some other exception thrown: $_[1]&quot; }
    when !*.defined, Nil { 'Undefined return value...' }
    default { &quot;Proper value: $_[0]&quot; }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Using the &quot;whatever star&quot; for matching quite literally whatever for the first position when there is an exception, and using the custom predicate <code>!*.defined</code> (this is a regular feature called &quot;whatever priming&quot;) to spot undefined values not caused by <code>try</code> (the exception is <code>Nil</code>).</p>
<p>The good thing about this pattern is that it's really easy to separate even <code>Failure</code>s and <code>Exception</code>s by changing <code>try { might-go-wonky() }</code> to <code>try { no fatal; might-go-wonky() }</code> in which case the first value might be a <code>Failure</code> (a specific kind of undefined value) which can also be matched against.</p>
